          "documentId": "doc-24e395b9bb6e",
          "text": "Einstein was eventually awarded the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics “for his services to Theoretical Physics, and especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect”. ",
          "start": 249,
          "end": 429,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "Einstein was eventually awarded the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics “for his services to Theoretical Physics, and especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect”."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-46199fd9f0db::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-46199fd9f0db",
          "text": "According to the statutes, a reserved prize can be awarded the year after, and Albert Einstein was awarded the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922.\n\n",
          "start": 275,
          "end": 424,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "According to the statutes, a reserved prize can be awarded the year after, and Albert Einstein was awarded the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics in 1922."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-46199fd9f0db::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-46199fd9f0db",
          "text": "**Question:**_When was Albert Einstein awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics?",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 74,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "**Question:**_When was Albert Einstein awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics?"
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-46199fd9f0db::sentence-3",
          "documentId": "doc-46199fd9f0db",
          "text": "**Answer:** The Nobel Prize Awarding Institution, the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, decided to reserve the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921, and therefore no Physics Prize was awarded that year. ",
          "start": 77,
          "end": 275,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "**Answer:** The Nobel Prize Awarding Institution, the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences, decided to reserve the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1921, and therefore no Physics Prize was awarded that year."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-24e395b9bb6e::sentence-1",
          "documentId": "doc-24e395b9bb6e",
          "text": "In 1905, Albert Einstein published the first explanation of the photoelectric effect, but at the time, it was impossible to resolve the timescales that were relevant for this effect. ",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 183,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "In 1905, Albert Einstein published the first explanation of the photoelectric effect, but at the time, it was impossible to resolve the timescales that were relevant for this effect."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-24e395b9bb6e::sentence-4",
          "documentId": "doc-24e395b9bb6e",
          "text": "Parenthetically, when he gave his Nobel lecture, it was not in Stockholm in December 1922 (Einstein was in Japan at that time), but in the middle of the summer of 1923, in Gothenburg – a unique event in the history of the Nobel Prize. ",
          "start": 429,
          "end": 664,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-24e395b9bb6e::sentence-3",
              "documentId": "doc-24e395b9bb6e",
              "text": "Einstein was eventually awarded the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics “for his services to Theoretical Physics, and especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect”. ",
              "start": 249,
              "end": 429,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-24e395b9bb6e::sentence-5",
              "documentId": "doc-24e395b9bb6e",
              "text": "His talk did not concern the photoelectric effect but the theory of relativity, the theory for which he was never awarded a Nobel Prize. ",
              "start": 664,
              "end": 801,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Einstein was eventually awarded the 1921 Nobel Prize in Physics “for his services to Theoretical Physics, and especially for his discovery of the law of the photoelectric effect”. Parenthetically, when he gave his Nobel lecture, it was not in Stockholm in December 1922 (Einstein was in Japan at that time), but in the middle of the summer of 1923, in Gothenburg – a unique event in the history of the Nobel Prize. His talk did not concern the photoelectric effect but the theory of relativity, the theory for which he was never awarded a Nobel Prize."
        }
